<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<topic id="25a5da19-1b26-467d-9e5d-627b075e47c5" revisionNumber="1">
  <developerConceptualDocument
    xmlns="http://ddue.schemas.microsoft.com/authoring/2003/5"
    xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">

    <introduction>
      <definitionTable><definition>
                DemiCode Scheduler uses loggers to log messages and error information while running.
                Version 1.1 comes with a log4net logger and a console logger out of the box. If
                these loggers does not fit your needs you can easily implement your own. 
               </definition><definition>
               Loggers in Scheduler consist of (at least) three core classes:
               </definition>
               <definition>
               <list class="bullet">
               <listItem>A factory</listItem>
               <listItem>A logger</listItem>
               <listItem>An NDC</listItem>
               </list>
               </definition>
               <definition>
                This section describes how to implement these classes.
               </definition>
               </definitionTable>
    </introduction>

    
                                    

<section>
  <title>Implementing the factory</title>
  <content>
    <definitionTable>
                    <definition>
                        The logger factory is a class that "knows" how to create logger instances and NDC
                        (Nested Diagnostic Context) objects. The Scheduler keeps an instance of the factory
                        for whenever a logger needs to be created, thus the factory should be light-weight.
                        Equally, the Create methods should be as fast as possible.
                    </definition>
                    <definition>
                        The logger factory class must implement the
                        <codeEntityReference qualifyHint="false">T:DemiCode.Logging.ILoggerFactory</codeEntityReference>
                        interface. The following code shows how the
                        <codeEntityReference qualifyHint="false">T:DemiCode.Logging.ConsoleLoggerFactory</codeEntityReference>
                        is implemented.
					<code language="c#">
public class ConsoleLoggerFactory : ILoggerFactory
{
    ILogger ILoggerFactory.CreateLogger(string name)
    {
        return new ConsoleLogger();
    }

    ILogger ILoggerFactory.CreateLogger(Type type)
    {
        return new ConsoleLogger();
    }

    IDisposable ILoggerFactory.CreateNDC(string message)
    {
        return new SimpleNDC(message);
    }
}
					</code>
                    </definition>
                    <definition>
                        <legacyBold>Note:</legacyBold> this simplified implemenation does not use the name and type
                        parameters. These should be passed on to the logger instance to add contextual information.
                    </definition>
		</definitionTable>
  </content>
</section>
    
        
            

<section>
  <title>Implementing the logger</title>
  <content>
    <definitionTable>
                    <definition>
                        The logger must implement the
                        <codeEntityReference qualifyHint="false">T:DemiCode.Logging.ILogger</codeEntityReference>
                        interface. This interface consist of five groups of methods.
                        </definition>
                        <definition>
                        <list class="bullet">
                            <listItem>Debug</listItem>
                            <listItem>Information</listItem>
                            <listItem>Warning</listItem>
                            <listItem>Error</listItem>
                            <listItem>Fatal</listItem>
                        </list>
                        </definition>
                        <definition>
                        Each group is responsible for logging information at different priority levels.
                        Code that uses your logger will choose a level according to the importance of the
                        logged information. How the chosen level is expressed in the logger is up to you
                        as the implementer.
                    </definition>
                    <definition>
                        The following code shows how the
                        <codeEntityReference qualifyHint="false">T:DemiCode.Logging.ConsoleLogger</codeEntityReference>
                        is implemented. Note that each level, e.g. Fatal, have two methods. Both accept
                        a <legacyBold>message</legacyBold> and <legacyBold>args</legacyBold> parameter. The logger user
                        expect these to support string format notation. One overload accepts an <legacyBold>exception</legacyBold>
                        object which enables us to format exception information explicitly.
                    </definition>
                    <definition>
                        <code language="c#">
public class ConsoleLogger : ILogger
{
    void ILogger.Fatal(string message, params object[] args)
    {
        Console.BackgroundColor = ConsoleColor.Red;
        Console.WriteLine(message, args);
    }

    void ILogger.Fatal(string message, Exception exception, params object[] args)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(message, args);
        WriteException(exception);
    }

    void ILogger.Error(string message, params object[] args)
    {
        Console.BackgroundColor = ConsoleColor.Red;
        Console.WriteLine(message, args);
    }

    void ILogger.Error(string message, Exception exception, params object[] args)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(message, args);
        WriteException(exception);
    }

    void ILogger.Warn(string message, params object[] args)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(message, args);
    }

    void ILogger.Warn(string message, Exception exception, params object[] args)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(message, args);
        WriteException(exception);
    }

    void ILogger.Info(string message, params object[] args)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(message, args);
    }

    void ILogger.Info(string message, Exception exception, params object[] args)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(message, args);
        WriteException(exception);
    }

    void ILogger.Debug(string message, params object[] args)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(message, args);
    }

    void ILogger.Debug(string message, Exception exception, params object[] args)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(message, args);
        WriteException(exception);
    }

    private static void WriteException(Exception e)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(&quot;{0}: {1}&quot;, e.GetType().Name, e.Message);
        Console.WriteLine(e.StackTrace);
    }
}
               			</code>
                    </definition>
                </definitionTable>
  </content>
</section>
            
               
            
            
 
<section>
  <title>Implementing a simple NDC</title>
  <content>
    <definitionTable>
                    <definition>
                        A Nested Diagnostic Context object is used to provide additional context to loggers.
                        You can read more on <externalLink>
  <linkText>how log4net uses contexts</linkText>
  <linkUri>http://logging.apache.org/log4net/release/manual/contexts.html</linkUri>
  <linkTarget>_blank</linkTarget>
</externalLink>.
                    </definition>
                    <definition>
                        For our console logger we will implement a dummy NDC called SimpleNDC. The only
                        requirement for a Scheduler NDC is that it supports the IDisposable interface.
					<code language="c#">
public class SimpleNDC : IDisposable
{
    private string _message;
    public SimpleNDC(string message)
    {
        _message = message;
    }

    private bool _disposed = false;
    private void Dispose(bool disposing)
    {
        if (!_disposed)
        {
            _disposed = true;
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Dispose(true);
        GC.SuppressFinalize(this);
    }
}               
               		</code>
                    </definition>
	</definitionTable>
  </content>
</section>
               
            
            
 
<section>
  <title>Whats next?</title>
  <content>
    <para>Read up on how to enable <link xlink:href="2ae78a2b-2f6e-447c-9188-7483bd116e74">your logger in configuration</link> and then use the <link xlink:href="f0361e4e-a359-4f54-a200-6e8a11b94dff">SchedulerService</link> sample to try it out.</para>
  </content>
</section>
               
            
            
   

